<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meng Zhixiang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">874–<a href="/years/934">934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Gaozu of Later Shu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meng Zhixiang was the founder of Later Shu, one of the Ten Kingdoms of tenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A general of the Later Tang, he was appointed military governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> after the conquest of Former Shu in 925, gradually made himself independent, and in 934 declared himself emperor. He died months later and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-chang">Meng Chang</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Meng Zhixiang was born on 10 May 874 into a family of Xing Prefecture whose men had served for generations as army officers there; his uncles Meng Fangli and Meng Qian both governed Zhaoyi Circuit late in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. When Meng Qian surrendered Zhaoyi to Zhu Quanzhong in 901, Meng Zhixiang's father stayed with the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> of Hedong, who was impressed with the young man's talent, gave him his eldest daughter in marriage and made him a training officer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, Prince of Jin, he saved the official Li Yan from execution around 912, and around 919 declined the dangerous post of chief of staff of Tianxiong, recommending Guo Chongtao, who remained grateful. When Li Cunxu proclaimed the Later Tang in 923 as Emperor Zhuangzong, he made Meng mayor of the western capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>. In 925 Guo, leading the campaign against Former Shu, recommended him to govern Xichuan, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> fell the emperor appointed him military governor.</p><p>Under Emperor Mingzong, Meng prepared to hold the region himself, raising 36 more corps of some 32,000 soldiers beside the 27,000 Guo had left. When the chancellor Ren Huan sent Zhao Jiliang to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in winter 926, Meng let the stored wealth of Former Shu go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> but refused to hand over further taxes. Fearing the accusations of Mingzong's chief adviser An Chonghui, he rebelled in alliance with Dong Zhang of Dongchuan Circuit; they repelled attempts to subdue them, and Meng later overpowered Dong and held both domains as a nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Mingzong's reign, Meng declared himself emperor of Later Shu. Soon afterwards, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-congke">Li Congke</a> rebelled and seized the Later Tang throne, the military governors Zhang Qianzhao of Shannan West and Sun Hanshao of Wuding Circuit surrendered their circuits to him, giving Later Shu the region south of the Qinling Mountains. Meng had reportedly suffered a stroke for over a year; in autumn 934 he made his son Meng Renzan crown prince and died the same day, and the son, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-chang">Meng Chang</a>, took the throne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meng Zhixiang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Later Tang conquered Former Shu in 925, <b>Meng Zhixiang</b> was appointed military governor at Chengdu.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-shu">Later Shu</a></li></ul></section>
